---
title: "Force of gravity"
author: "Ožbej Kresal"
date: "June 2026"
---

In this exercise we implemented a Julia package which calculates the gravitational force between two parallel-placed unit homogeneous cubes at a distance of 1 (between the closest sides). Where the force between two bodies $T_1,T_2 \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ is equal to
$$
\mathbf{F}
=
\int_{T_1}
\int_{T_2}
\frac{\mathbf{r}_1-\mathbf{r}_2}
{\left\|\mathbf{r}_1-\mathbf{r}_2\right\|_2^3}
\, d\mathbf{r}_1 \, d\mathbf{r}_2.
$$

To do that we used the Gauss-Legendre quadrature, where we implemented both the calculation of nodes and weights using the Golub-Welsch algorithm.

# Gravitational Force Integral
We have to calculate the gravitational force between two parallel-placed unit homogeneous cubes at a distance of 1. 

We define the cubes as:

$$
T_1 = [0,1] \times [0,1] \times [0,1],
\qquad
T_2 = [2,3] \times [0,1] \times [0,1].
$$

The second cube is shifted by 2 units in the $x$ direction, so that the gap between the cubes is exactly 1.

We then expand the gravitational force integral:

$$
\mathbf{F}
=
\int_{T_1}
\int_{T_2}
\frac{\mathbf{r}_1 - \mathbf{r}_2}{\|\mathbf{r}_1 - \mathbf{r}_2\|^3}
\, d\mathbf{r}_2 \, d\mathbf{r}_1
$$

where

$$
\mathbf{r}_1 = (x_1, y_1, z_1), \quad \mathbf{r}_2 = (x_2, y_2, z_2),
$$

into Cartesian coordinates which gives us the following six dimensional integral:

$$
\mathbf{F} =
\int_0^1 \int_0^1 \int_0^1 \int_2^3 \int_0^1 \int_0^1
\frac{
(x_1 - x_2,\; y_1 - y_2,\; z_1 - z_2)}
{\left[(x_1 - x_2)^2 + (y_1 - y_2)^2 + (z_1 - z_2)^2\right]^{3/2}}
\, dx_2\, dy_2\, dz_2\, dx_1\, dy_1\, dz_1
$$

Since the cubes are parallel and differ only in the $x$ direction the force will therefore be:

$$
\mathbf{F} =
\int_0^1 \int_0^1 \int_0^1 \int_2^3 \int_0^1 \int_0^1
\frac{x_1 - x_2,}
{\left[(x_1 - x_2)^2 + (y_1 - y_2)^2 + (z_1 - z_2)^2\right]^{3/2}}
\, dx_2\, dy_2\, dz_2\, dx_1\, dy_1\, dz_1
$$

# Gauss-Legendre quadrature

To calculate the 6 dimensional integral we used the Gauss-Legendre quadrature, which approximates a definite integral by a weighted sum

$$
\int_a^b f(x)\,dx \approx \sum_{i=1}^n w_i f(x_i)
$$

where $x_i$ are the quadrature nodes and $w_i$ are the corresponding weights.


## Computing the nodes and weights

To find the nodes and weights we used the Golub-Welsch algorithm, which finds them as eigenvalues of the following tridiagonal matrix (Jacobi matrix)
$$
J =
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & b_1 & 0 & \cdots & 0 \\
b_1 & 0 & b_2 & \cdots & 0 \\
0 & b_2 & 0 & \ddots & \vdots \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \ddots & b_{n-1} \\
0 & 0 & \cdots & b_{n-1} & 0
\end{pmatrix}
$$

where

$$
b_i = \frac{i}{\sqrt{4i^2 - 1}}.
$$

The eigenvalues $\lambda_i$ of this matrix give the quadrature nodes, and the weights are computed from the first component of the normalized eigenvectors $v_{1i}$:

$$
x_i\, w_i = 2 (v_{1i})^2.
$$
```julia, eval = false
function my_gausslegendre(n)
    diagonal = zeros(n)
    subdiagonal = [i / sqrt(4i^2 - 1) for i in 1:(n-1)]
    J = SymTridiagonal(diagonal, subdiagonal)
    x, V = eigen(J)
    w = 2.0 .* (V[1, :].^2)
    return x, w
end
```

# Demonstration

```julia
using dn2, Plots
n = 10
force_n = dn2.force(10)
rounded_force_n = round(force_n, digits=10)
println("Calculated force between two unit cubes one unit apart rounded to 10 digits: $rounded_force_n")

force_n1 = dn2.force(11)
error = abs(force_n1 - force_n)
println("\nCalculated error: $error")

println("Plotting error drop off as we increase the number of nodes...")
ns = 2:n
vals = Float64[]
for _n in ns
  push!(vals, dn2.force(_n))
end

plot(ns, abs.(vals .- force_n1), ylabel="Error", xlabel="Number of nodes", yscale=:log10, marker=:circle, legend=false, yticks=10.0 .^ (0:-5:-15))
```
